\f2\b \cf3 Whose eyes, and the tone
\f3\b0 \cf3 \
Cut between the people above, one at a time. (log \'a7N) Comic and satirical; the concert's a real win, but keep the edge \'97 trim the sentiment (tears in every eye, "fellow lottery winners"). The irony at the end is the tone to protect. Sarah's and Chanta's parts carry the ache. [mine \'97 tone]\

\f2\b \cf3 What must not slip out
\f3\b0 \cf3 \
Chanta doesn't know what he is; nothing about the virus, or what Sarah really is. The baby stirring is a tender moment \'97 don't let it tip into anything that explains the child. (log \'97 secret)\

\f2\b \cf3 What it pays off and sets up
\f3\b0 \cf3 \
Pays off: "Religious Minds" landing on half a million; the Elvi answering the gospel with music; Sarah back before the Elvi as the Holy Momma, echoing Vegas. (log/canon) Sets up: the Get Bent Street sit-in this sparks, and Dunhilton/Gerry moving on the virus. (log)\

\f2\b \cf3 Keep from the draft
\f3\b0 \cf3  [mine]\
The class-mixing spectacle; Dunhilton choking on artisanal water; the ironic Dawkins chant at the end; Humble's flock secretly humming "religious minds"; the songs. Fix the geography; trim the sentiment.\

